L000699	\section{S1. Derivation of heat fluxes}
L000700	\label{sec:S1}
L000701	
L000702	\subsection{General formalism}
L000703	
L000704	Nuclear spin Hamiltonian consists of two terms
L000705	\begin{equation}
L000706	  \mathcal H_n=\mathcal H_Z+\mathcal H_{SS},
L000707	\end{equation}
L000708	where $\mathcal H_Z=-\hbar\gamma_NBJ_z$ is the Zeeman Hamiltonian with $\gamma_N$ being the nuclear gyromagnetic ratio, $B$ being external magnetic field applied along the $z$ axis, and $\bm J=\sum_{i=1}^N\bm I_i$ being the total nuclear spin in the quantum dot composed of $N$ individual nuclear spins $\bm I_i$ (their absolute values $I$ are all equal), and $\mathcal H_{SS}$ is the Hamiltonian of the nuclear spin-spin interactions. Before arrival of an electron to the quantum dot, its density matrix reads
L000709	\begin{equation}
L000710	  \label{eq:rho0_S}
L000711	  \rho_0=1-\beta\mathcal H_n,
L000712	\end{equation}
L000713	where $\beta$ is an inverse nuclear spin temperature measured in the inverse energy units and the normalization $\Tr\rho_0=1$ is implicitly assumed.
L000714	
L000715	When an electron with the spin $\bm S$ comes to the quantum dot, additional terms appear in the total Hamiltonian [Eq.~\eqref{eq:interaction} in the main text]:
L000716	\begin{equation}
L000717	  \label{eq:H_S}
L000718	  \mathcal H=\mathcal H_n+\hbar\gamma_eBS_z+\mathcal H_{hf}
L000719	\end{equation}
L000720	with $\gamma_e$ being the electron gyromagnetic ratio and
L000721	\begin{equation}
L000722	  \mathcal H_{hf}=a\bm J\bm S
L000723	\end{equation}
L000724	being the Hamiltonian of the homogeneous hyperfine interaction (``box approximation'') with a hyperfine coupling constant $a$.
L000725	
L000726	Then the evolution of the nuclear spin density matrix is described by
L000727	\begin{equation}
L000728	  \label{eq:rho_t}
L000729	  \rho(t)=\e^{-\i\mathcal H t/\hbar}\rho_0\e^{\i\mathcal H t/\hbar}.
L000730	\end{equation}
L000731	In particular, energy of the nuclear spin system is
L000732	\begin{equation}
L000733	  \label{eq:En}
L000734	  E_n(t)=\Tr\left[\mathcal H_n\rho(t)\right].
L000735	\end{equation}
L000736	An electron stays in the quantum dot for a typical time $\tau_c$, so the average change of the energy is given by
L000737	\begin{equation}
L000738	  \label{eq:dE_av}
L000739	  \braket{\Delta E_n}=\int\limits_0^\infty E_n(t)\e^{-t/\tau_c}\frac{\d t}{\tau_c}-E_n(0).
L000740	\end{equation}
L000741	
L000742	The energy flux can be derived from Eq.~\eqref{eq:dE_av} as $q=\braket{\Delta E_n}/\tau_R$, where $\tau_R$ is the typical electron arrival time to the quantum dot. The different terms in $q$ are introduced in Eq.~\eqref{eq:balance} in the main text. Let us calculate them explicitly.
L000743	
L000744	\subsection{Zeeman reservoir}
L000745	
L000746	Heating and cooling of the nuclear Zeeman reservoir takes place at the time scale of the electron spin dephasing time $T_{2,e}^*$. It is described by Eq.~\eqref{eq:dE_av} neglecting $\mathcal H_{SS}$. Then Eq.~\eqref{eq:H_S} reduces to the central spin Hamiltonian, which is already well studied~\cite{S_book_Glazov}.
L000747	
L000748	In particular, the electron spin dynamics is described by
L000749	\begin{equation}
L000750	  \label{eq:dS}
L000751	  \frac{\d\bm S}{\d t}=\bm\Omega_e\times\bm S,
L000752	\end{equation}
L000753	where $\bm\Omega_e=\bm\Omega_B+\bm\Omega_N$ is the electron spin precession frequency contributed by the external magnetic field $\bm\Omega_B=\gamma_e\bm B$ and nuclear Overhauser field $\bm\Omega_N=a\bm J/\hbar$. At the timescale $t\ll\hbar/a$ the nuclear spins are almost frozen, so the electron spin precesses with almost constant but random frequency~\cite{S_merkulov02}. The corresponding solution of Eq.~\eqref{eq:dS}, $\bm S_0(t)$, reads
L000754	\begin{multline}
L000755	  \label{eq:S0}
L000756	  \bm S_0(t)=\left[\bm S(0)\bm n\right]\bm n+\left\{\bm S(0)-\left[\bm S(0)\bm n\right]\bm n\right\}\cos\left(\Omega_e t\right)\\
L000757	  +\left[\bm S(0)\times\bm n\right]\sin\left(\Omega_e t\right),
L000758	\end{multline}
L000759	where $\bm n=\bm\Omega_e/\Omega_e$.
L000760	
L000761	The longitudinal electron spin relaxation time $T_{1,e}^N$ can be found from Eq.~\eqref{eq:S0} by considering $\bm S(0)$ parallel to the $z$ axis and averaging over nuclear spin distribution~\eqref{eq:rho0_S}:
L000762	\begin{equation}
L000763	  \braket{S_{0,z}(t)}=S(0)\braket{\frac{\Omega_{e,z}^2}{\Omega_e^2}+\frac{\Omega_{e,\perp}^2}{\Omega_e^2}\cos\left(\Omega_et\right)},
L000764	\end{equation}
L000765	where $\Omega_{e,\perp}^2=\Omega_{e,x}^2+\Omega_{e,y}^2$. Now the time $T_{1,e}^N$ can be found from
L000766	\begin{equation}
L000767	  \label{eq:T1e_def}
L000768	  \frac{S(0)}{T_{1,e}^N}=\frac{1}{\tau_c}\int\left[S(0)-\braket{S_{0,z}(t)}\right]\e^{-t/\tau_c}\frac{\d t}{\tau_c}
L000769	\end{equation}
L000770	and reads~\cite{S_Glazov_hopping,S_PRC}
L000771	\begin{equation}
L000772	  \label{eq:T1e}
L000773	  \frac{1}{T_{1,e}^N}=\braket{\frac{\Omega_{e,\perp}^2\tau_c}{1+\Omega_e^2\tau_c^2}}.
L000774	\end{equation}
L000775	For short electron correlation time, this gives
L000776	\begin{equation}
L000777	  T_{1,e}^N=2T_{2,e}^{*2}(1+\Omega_B^2\tau_c^2)/\tau_c,
L000778	\end{equation}
L000779	where
L000780	\begin{equation}
L000781	  T_{2,e}^{*}=\frac{\hbar}{a\sqrt{N_I}}
L000782	\end{equation}
L000783	is the typical period of electron spin precession in the Overhauser field fluctuations with $N_I=4NI(I+1)/3$, which reduces to $N$ for $I=1/2$. In the opposite limit of long correlation time, $\tau_c\gg T_{2,e}^*$, the longitudinal electron spin relaxation time is
L000784	\begin{equation}
L000785	  T_{1,e}^N=\tau_c/\braket{\Omega_{e,\perp}^2/\Omega_e^2},
L000786	\end{equation}
L000787	so it is of the order of $\tau_c$ in small magnetic fields and increases as $B^2$ in strong magnetic fields. This dependence can be well approximated (with accuracy of a few percent~\cite{S_petrov08,S_book_Glazov}) by a Lorentzian:
L000788	\begin{equation}
L000789	  \frac{1}{T_{1,e}^N}=\frac{2}{3\tau_c}\frac{1}{1+\Omega_B^2T_{2,e}^{*2}}.
L000790	\end{equation}
L000791	These expressions describe the left hand side of Eq.~\eqref{eq:balance} as well as Eq.~\eqref{eq:q_Z} in the main text.
L000792	
L000793	This can be shown more rigorously by considering equations of the nuclear spin dynamics, which follows from Eq.~\eqref{eq:H_S}:
L000794	\begin{equation}
L000795	  \label{eq:dI}
L000796	  \frac{\d\bm J}{\d t}=\bm\omega_N\times\bm J,
L000797	\end{equation}
L000798	where $\bm\omega_N=-\gamma_N\bm B+a\bm S/\hbar$ is the nuclear spin precession frequency in the sum of external magnetic and Knight fields. The nuclear Zeeman energy is $E_Z=-\hbar\gamma_NBJ_z$, and from comparison of Eqs.~\eqref{eq:dS} and~\eqref{eq:dI} one can see that $\d J_z/\d t=-\d S_z/\d t$ due to the conservation of the total angular momentum component along $\bm B$ in the central spin model. So the change of $E_Z$ (in the main order in $1/\sqrt{N}$) is
L000799	\begin{equation}
L000800	  \Delta E_Z(t)=\hbar\gamma_NB\left[S_{0,z}(t)-S(0)\right].
L000801	\end{equation}
L000802	Now comparing this with Eqs.~\eqref{eq:T1e_def} and~\eqref{eq:dE_av} we obtain the energy flux from the nuclear Zeeman reservoir
L000803	\begin{equation}
L000804	  \frac{f\braket{S_z}}{T_{1,e}^N}\hbar\gamma_N B
L000805	\end{equation}
L000806	in agreement with Eq.~\eqref{eq:balance} in the main text. Here $f=\tau_c/\tau_R$ is the average occupancy of the quantum dot with electrons and $\braket{S_z}$ is the average spin of hopping electrons along the $z$ axis. This determines the nuclear spin cooling rate.
L000807	
L000808	Heating of the nuclear Zeeman reservoir takes place in the next order in $1/\sqrt{N}$. To derive it, we consider a correction $\delta\bm S$ to Eq.~\eqref{eq:S0} due to the time dependence of $\bm\Omega_N(t)$. From Eq.~\eqref{eq:dS} one can see that it satisfies
L000809	\begin{equation}
L000810	  \label{eq:d_delta_S}
L000811	  \frac{\d\delta\bm S}{\d t}=\bm\Omega_e(0)\times\delta\bm S+\delta\bm\Omega_N\times\bm S_0,
L000812	\end{equation}
L000813	where $\delta\bm\Omega_N=\bm\Omega_N(t)-\bm\Omega_N(0)$. It can be found from the equation for the nuclear spin dynamics~\eqref{eq:dI}. Change of the nuclear Zeeman energy takes place at the time scale of $T_{2,e}^*$ when the nuclear spin rotation angle is small. Then $\delta\bm J=\bm J(t)-\bm J(0)$ can be calculated by integrating Eq.~\eqref{eq:dI} neglecting the time dependence of $\bm J$ in the right-hand side. Then we substitute $\delta\bm\Omega_N=(a/\hbar)\delta\bm J$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:d_delta_S} and take into account smallness of the average electron spin polarization. Further we solve this equation for $\delta\bm S(t)$ making use of Eq.~\eqref{eq:S0}. Finally, the average correction to the change of the electron spin is
L000814	\begin{equation}
L000815	  \braket{\delta\bm S}=\braket{\int\limits_0^\infty\delta\bm S(t)\frac{\e^{-t/\tau_c}}{\tau_c}\d t}.
L000816	\end{equation}
L000817	While $\braket{\delta S_{x,y}}=0$, we obtain
L000818	\begin{equation}
L000819	  \label{eq:delta_S1}
L000820	  \braket{\delta S_z}=\braket{\frac{a^2\tau_c^2\left[J_z\left(1+\Omega_{e,z}^2\tau_c^2\right)+\hbar\Omega_{e,z}\Omega_{e,\perp}^2\tau_c^2/a\right]}{2\hbar^2\left(1+\Omega_e^2\tau_c^2\right)^2}}.
L000821	\end{equation}
L000822	The intermediate expressions are very cumbersome.
L000823	
L000824	The averaging in Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_S1} should be performed with the nuclear spin density matrix~\eqref{eq:rho0_S}. It corresponds to the distribution function of the Overhauser field
L000825	\begin{equation}
L000826	  \mathcal F(\bm\Omega_N)=\left(\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}T_{2,e}^*\right)^3\exp\left[-2T_{2,e}^{*2}\left(\bm\Omega_N-\braket{\bm\Omega_N}\right)^2\right]
L000827	\end{equation}
L000828	with the average Overhauser field $\braket{\bm\Omega_N}=a\braket{\bm J}/\hbar$ along the $z$ axis, where $\braket{\bm J}=N\braket{\bm I}$ is the average nuclear spin determined by the inverse nuclear spin temperature [Eq.~\eqref{eq:P_N} in the main text]
L000829	\begin{equation}
L000830	  \braket{I_z}=\frac{I(I+1)}{3}\hbar\gamma_NB\beta.
L000831	\end{equation}
L000832	Then one can show that Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_S1} is equivalent to
L000833	\begin{equation}
L000834	  \label{eq:delta_S_ans}
L000835	  \braket{\delta S_z}=\frac{\braket{J_z}}{N_I}\braket{\frac{\Omega_{e,\perp}^2\tau_c^2}{1+\Omega_e^2\tau_c^2}}.
L000836	\end{equation}
L000837	Due to the conservation of the total angular momentum component along the $z$ axis, the average nuclear spin decreases by the same value $\braket{\delta S_z}$. Thus, the nuclear spin relaxation time (by electrons) $T_{1,N}^e$ is determined by the relation
L000838	\begin{equation}
L000839	  \frac{\braket{J_z}}{T_{1,N}^e}=\frac{\braket{\delta S_z}}{\tau_R}.
L000840	\end{equation}
L000841	Substituting here Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_S_ans} and comparing this with Eq.~\eqref{eq:T1e} we obtain
L000842	\begin{equation}
L000843	  \label{eq:relation}
L000844	  T_{1,N}^e=\frac{N_I}{f}T_{1,e}^N.
L000845	\end{equation}
L000846	Finally, the nuclear Zeeman reservoir heating rate is given by
L000847	\begin{equation}
L000848	  q_Z=\frac{N\braket{I_z}}{T_{1,N}^e}\hbar\gamma_N B.
L000849	\end{equation}
L000850	With the help of Eq.~\eqref{eq:relation}, it gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:q_Z} in the main text, which was obtained there from the general considerations.
L000851	
L000852	\subsection{Spin-spin reservoir}
L000853	
L000854	Now let us calculate the heating rate $q_{SS}$ of the nuclear spin-spin reservoir. Since Eq.~\eqref{eq:En} vanishes in the first order in $\mathcal H_{SS}$, it is necessary to consider the second order. It reads
L000855	\begin{multline}
L000856	  \label{eq:ESS_general}
L000857	  E_{SS}(t)=-\beta\Tr\Bigg\{\mathcal H_{SS}\mathcal H_{SS}(t)\\
L000858	  +\frac{\i}{\hbar}\int\limits_0^t\d t'\left(\mathcal H_{SS}\left[\mathcal H_{SS}(t'),\mathcal H_Z(t)\right]+\left[\mathcal H_Z,\mathcal H_{SS}(t')\right]\mathcal H_{SS}(t)\right)\\
L000859	  +\frac{1}{\hbar^2}\int\limits_0^t\d t'\int\limits_{t'}^t\d t''\left[\mathcal H_Z,\mathcal H_{SS}(t')\right]\left[\mathcal H_Z(t),\mathcal H_{SS}(t'')\right]\Bigg\},
L000860	\end{multline}
L000861	where the time dependence represents the evolution under action of the Hamiltonian $\mathcal H_0=\mathcal H_Z+\hbar\gamma_eBS_z+\mathcal H_{hf}$.
L000862	
L000863	At the short time scales $t\ll\hbar/a$, the nuclear spin rotation angles are small so one can consider the fourth order in $\mathcal H$ only. One can check readily that this is equivalent to
L000864	\begin{multline}
L000865	  \label{eq:ESS_short}
L000866	  E_{SS}(t)=-\beta\Tr\left\{\mathcal H_{SS}\mathcal T\exp\left[\frac{\i}{\hbar}\int\limits_0^t\mathcal H_{hf}(t')\d t'\right]
L000867	  \right.\\\left.
L000868	    \times\mathcal H_{SS}\overline{\mathcal T}\exp\left[-\frac{\i}{\hbar}\int\limits_0^t\mathcal H_{hf}(t')\d t'\right]\right\},
L000869	\end{multline}
L000870	where $\mathcal T$ and $\overline{\mathcal T}$ denote time ordering and its reverse. This equation shows that for the calculation of the nuclear spin-spin energy, one can consider only the nuclear spin dynamics under $\mathcal H_{hf}(t)$, without additional spin precession due to the Zeeman term. Physically this is related to the facts that $\mathcal H_Z$ enters already the steady state density matrix~\eqref{eq:rho0_S} and that the small nuclear spin rotation angles commute.
L000871	
L000872	Substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq:S0} in Eq.~\eqref{eq:ESS_short} we obtain
L000873	\begin{equation}
L000874	  \label{eq:delta_ESS}
L000875	  \braket{\Delta E_{SS}}=\frac{\beta(\hbar\gamma_N\tilde B_L\tau_c)^2}{24T_{2,e}^*}\left(1+\braket{\frac{2}{1+\Omega_e^2\tau_c^2}}\right),
L000876	\end{equation}
L000877	where
L000878	\begin{equation}
L000879	  {\tilde B}_L^2=-\frac{2}{N_I(\hbar\gamma_N)^2}\Tr\left[\bm J,\mathcal H_{SS}\right]^2.
L000880	\end{equation}
L000881	For dipole-dipole interaction, it equals $\sqrt{3}B_L$~\cite{S_abragam,S_OptOrCh2}, where the local nuclear field $B_L$ is defined by
L000882	\begin{equation}
L000883	  B_L^2=\frac{4}{N_I(\hbar\gamma_N)^2}\Tr\mathcal H_{SS}^2.
L000884	\end{equation}
L000885	From comparison of Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_ESS} and Eq.~\eqref{eq:q_SS} in the main text, we obtain Eq.~\eqref{eq:Phi_ans} in the main text.
L000886	
L000887	Calculation of the change of the nuclear spin-spin energy for long times $t\gtrsim\hbar/a$ after Eq.~\eqref{eq:ESS_general} is difficult. But neglecting the nuclear Zeeman splitting in the spin dynamics, one arrives again at Eq.~\eqref{eq:ESS_short}, which generally describes rotation of all nuclear spins by the same angle $\varphi$ around some axis. Then, we obtain the corresponding nuclear spin-spin energy
L000888	\begin{equation}
L000889	  \label{eq:ESS_phi}
L000890	  E_{SS}=-\frac{N_I\beta}{20}\left[1+2\cos(\varphi)+2\cos(2\varphi)\right]\left(\hbar\gamma_NB_L\right)^2.
L000891	\end{equation}
L000892	For short correlation time, one has $\phi=at/(2\hbar)$ and Eq.~\eqref{eq:ESS_phi} reproduces Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_ESS} with $\Omega_e\tau_c\gg 1$. By contrast, for long correlation time, when $at/(2\hbar)\gg 1$ one can neglect the oscillating terms in Eq.~\eqref{eq:ESS_phi}, which gives
L000893	\begin{equation}
L000894	  \label{eq:Phi_long}
L000895	  \Phi=\frac{4}{5}\left(\frac{\hbar}{a\tau_c}\right)^2
L000896	\end{equation}
L000897	in a qualitative agreement with the general considerations in the main text.
L000898	
L000899	\begin{figure*}
L000900	  \includegraphics[width=0.49\linewidth]{RotB}
L000901	  \includegraphics[width=0.49\linewidth]{RotBeta}
L000902	  \caption{(a) Optimal magnetic field $B_0$ at which the maximum of the inverse nuclear spin temperature $\beta_{\rm max}$ is reached as a function of the correlation time $\tau_c$. (b) $\beta_{\rm max}$ as a function of $\tau_c$. The solid lines are calculated after Eq.~\eqref{eq:beta} in the main text using Eq.~\eqref{eq:phi_simple}, they are the same as in Fig.~\ref{fig:combo}(c,d) in the main text. The dots are calculated accounting for the electron-nuclear spin dynamics described by Eq.~\eqref{eq:omega_n}. Red and blue colors correspond to $N_I=10^6$ and $10^4$, respectively.
L000903	  }
L000904	  \label{fig:rot}
L000905	\end{figure*}
L000906	
L000907	Another exact result can be found for the case of $\Omega_BT_{2,e}^*\gg1$, when external magnetic field is much stronger than the fluctuations of the Overhauser field (average Overhauser field can be added to $\Omega_B$ here). Then Eq.~\eqref{eq:ESS_general} yields
L000908	\begin{multline}
L000909	  \label{eq:ESS_complicated}
L000910	  E_{SS}(t)=-\beta\Tr\Big[\mathcal H_{SS}^2-\frac{1}{2}\sum_\pm\left(\frac{\omega_e}{\omega_e\pm\omega_B}\right)^2\Big(\mathcal H_{SS}^2\\
L000911	  -\mathcal H_{SS}\e^{\i(\omega_e\pm\omega_B)J_zt}\mathcal H_{SS}\e^{-\i(\omega_e\pm\omega_B)J_zt}\Big)\Big],
L000912	\end{multline}
L000913	where $\omega_e=a/(2\hbar)$ and $\omega_B=-\gamma_NB$ are the nuclear spin precession frequencies in the Knight and external magnetic fields, respectively. One can see that for $\omega_B=0$ this agrees again with Eqs.~\eqref{eq:ESS_short} and~\eqref{eq:ESS_phi}, where $\phi=at/(2\hbar)$. Then its average gives
L000914	\begin{equation}
L000915	  \Phi=\frac{2}{5}\left(\frac{\hbar}{a\tau_c}\right)^2\left(2-\frac{1}{1+(a\tau_c/\hbar)^2}-\frac{4}{4+(a\tau_c/\hbar)^2}\right).
L000916	\end{equation}
L000917	This expression represents an interpolation between Eq.~\eqref{eq:Phi_ans} in the main text and Eq.~\eqref{eq:Phi_long}.
L000918	
L000919	Fig.~\ref{fig:rot} shows comparison of the simplified calculation with
L000920	\begin{multline}
L000921	  \label{eq:phi_simple}
L000922	  \Phi=
L000923	  \left<\frac{1}{1+\Omega_e^2\tau_c^2}\right>+\\
L000924	  \frac{2}{5}\left(\frac{\hbar}{a\tau_c}\right)^2\left(2-\frac{1}{1+(a\tau_c/\hbar)^2}-\frac{4}{4+(a\tau_c/\hbar)^2}\right),
L000925	\end{multline}
L000926	which was shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:combo} in the main text (solid lines) and exact calculation based on Eq.~\eqref{eq:ESS_phi} (dots). In the latter case, the nuclear spin dynamics was described using an exact solution obtained in Ref.~\onlinecite{S_PhysRevLett.126.216804}. It represents rotation of average electron and nuclear spins around external magnetic field with the frequency
L000927	\begin{equation}
L000928	  \label{eq:omega_n}
L000929	  \omega_n=\omega_e\frac{\Omega_B}{\Omega_e}
L000930	\end{equation}
L000931	neglecting the nuclear Zeeman splitting ($\omega_B=0$). One can see almost perfect agreement between the two calculations.
L000932	
L000933	In the opposite limit of $\omega_B\gg\omega_e$ and large nuclear spin rotation angles, the change of the nuclear spin-spin energy in Eq.~\eqref{eq:ESS_complicated} is suppressed by a factor $(\omega_e/\omega_B)^2$ because of the contribution of $\mathcal H_Z$ to the initial nuclear spin density matrix~\eqref{eq:rho0_S}. Depending on the electron localization strength and nuclear gyromagnetic ratio, this may lead to appearance of a second maximum in the dependence $\beta(B)$ at higher magnetic fields, when heating of the nuclear spin-spin reservoir gets suppressed.
L000934	
L000935	\section{S2. Comparison of $T_{2,N}$ and $T_{1,N}^e$}
L000936	
L000937	Establishment of the nuclear spin temperature requires the thermalization time $T_{2,N}$ to be shorter than the nuclear spin polarization time by localized electrons $T_{1,N}^e$ given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:relation}. The latter increases with increase of magnetic field and nonmonotonously depends on the correlation time $\tau_c$. It reaches the minimum of approximately $4.9N_IT_{2,e}^*/f$ at $\tau_c\approx0.88T_{2,e}^*$. In GaAs-based quantum dots, typically $aN\sim100~\mu$eV and $T_{2,N}\sim0.1$~ms. Therefore the nuclear spin temperatures establishes at $N^{3/2}/f>10^7$. This condition is always satisfied for $N=10^6$, while for $N=10^4$ it requires $f<0.1$. This shows that the nuclear spin temperature is established in most of experiments.
L000938	
L000939	The strong magnetic field or Overhauser field may prevent thermalization, but experiments with quantum dots do not show this~\cite{S_Chekhovich_constants,S_PhysRevB.97.041301}, probably due to the inhomogeneity of the hyperfine interaction and strain.
